WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.649
These columns come from one pinned source snapshot.

2
00:00:04.649 --> 00:00:09.481
The CV32A60X example is 32-bit and machine-mode only.

3
00:00:09.481 --> 00:00:15.133
It has no MMU, disables the F and D floating-point extensions,

4
00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:18.506
and sets the PMP-entry count to zero.

5
00:00:18.506 --> 00:00:22.426
The RV64, Sv39 example enables translation,

6
00:00:22.426 --> 00:00:29.354
three privilege modes and floating-point extensions, with eight PMP entries.

7
00:00:29.354 --> 00:00:37.285
M, S and U mean machine, supervisor and user mode, distinguishing execution privileges.

8
00:00:37.285 --> 00:00:43.393
Shared source can therefore produce different feature combinations.

9
00:00:43.393 --> 00:00:47.587
Selecting an MMU is about address translation;

10
00:00:47.587 --> 00:00:54.059
enabling floating point is about the instructions software can execute.

11
00:00:54.059 --> 00:00:59.894
These are separate decisions, not a single advanced-core switch.

12
00:00:59.894 --> 00:01:03.358
You do not need to memorize the table.

13
00:01:03.358 --> 00:01:10.377
Ask what your software requires and what the configuration actually supplies.

14
00:01:10.377 --> 00:01:18.309
The explorer also offers a dual-issue example; issue width is not measured performance.

15
00:01:18.309 --> 00:01:24.599
The CV32A60X TRL5 release discussed later has a separate named scope.

16
00:01:24.599 --> 00:01:29.521
Keep that release distinct from these source examples.

17
00:01:29.521 --> 00:01:36.906
The right column is specifically cv64a6_imafdc_sv39, not all RV64 configurations.

18
00:01:36.906 --> 00:01:41.646
Zero PMP entries does not mean no system protection.

19
00:01:41.646 --> 00:01:46.842
Shared source does not make arbitrary combinations valid;

20
00:01:46.842 --> 00:01:54.500
implementation parameters alone do not establish full architectural CSR conformance.

